speechtext:
We certainly do not try to ram business through in one day, whether it is unemployment insurance or anything else, and try to finish estimates, involving the spending of $4 billion, in 20 days.
Mr. Pallet!: The hon. member who introduced the bill belongs to a party which enjoys the confidence of the people of one province. I think it would be of interest to put on the record the type of legislation they have in that province with respect to minimum wages. As I understand it, in Saskatchewan there are two general orders, one applicable to all establishments not covered by other orders in the cities and the 15 larger towns and one applicable to such establishments in the remainder of the province. These orders set rates of $30 and $29 a week respectively for full-time adult workers and fix lower rates for employees under 18 years of age.
In addition, there are nine special orders covering specific groups of workers. The coverage of the special orders and the rates set for full-time experienced workers are as follows: hotels and restaurants in cities and the 15 larger towns, $30 per week; hotels and restaurants in the remainder of the province, $29 per week. For educational institutions, hospitals and nursing homes in cities and the 15 larger towns the rate is $30 per week; for educational institutions, hospitals and nursing homes in the remainder of the province it is $29 per week. Janitors and caretakers in residential buildings have a minimum rate of $40 per week. For truck drivers the rate is 85 cents per hour or 3 cents per mile, whichever is the greater. For logging and lumbering the rate is 70 cents per hour. For amusement places it is $30 per week and for oil well drilling rigs it is 70 cents per hour.
I suggest, Mr. Speaker, that as the government formed by the party to which the hon. member who introduced the bill belongs recognizes in a single province that it cannot

legislate right across the board for an hourly-rate, we too should recognize the limitations in attempting to establish across this wide dominion one particular rate to apply to all classes of persons. It must be recognized that in certain areas of the country fuel bills are higher. In other areas there are higher transportation costs. In other areas rents are higher. All of these factors must be taken into consideration.
I suggest that my friend could have improved the bill if he had followed the pattern, to which I point particularly with a certain amount of pride, laid down in my own province of Ontario as set out in the minimum wage act. I refer to the establishment of a board which may hold hearings in certain areas and negotiate and define minimum wages for those areas. For the hon. member's information I should like to read section 2 of the act. It is in these words:
The board may arrange for a conference between employers and employees or their representatives in any business, trade, work, undertaking or occupation for the purpose of obtaining information as to the prevailing rates of wages and conditions of employment and may accept recommendations from the conference with respect to any matters which may be dealt with by an order of the board.
Section 3 of that act reads as follows:
The board may establish minimum rates of wages for all employees and generally enact such provisions with respect to conditions of employment as may be deemed necessary for the betterment of the physical, moral and intellectual wellbeing of employees and without restricting the generality of the foregoing the board may make orders and by means of such orders may,
(a)	designate or define any business, trade, work or undertaking or the part or parts thereof to which the order is applicable;
(b)	designate or define the zone or zones within Ontario in which any order or part thereof is applicable;
(c)	classify employees and separately provide for any one or more classifications with respect to any matter over which the board has authority;
(d)	establish a minimum wage for the prevailing weekly work period in the business of any employer or for any other working period which the board may establish;
(e)	establish the maximum number of hours of labour which may regularly be worked in the business of any employer with respect to any minimum wage established.
(f)	establish minimum hourly rates of wages for overtime work;
(g)	establish minimum hourly rates of wages for employees who regularly work less than 40 hours per week;
(h)	define any term used in any order;
(i)	establish a special method of payment for any classification of employees;
(j)	specify when and under what conditions deductions may be made from the minimum wage established for time lost by employees through illness, holidays, absence from duty or for any other reason and also for special privileges or perquisites resulting from the nature of the work performed.
